On-disk layout (a FRESH format -- `mb-run/2` etc.; no compatibility with the
node stack's archives is claimed or wanted, ruling 7):

    <runs-root>/
      archive.json                 the equal-height champion archive, shared
                                   by every run under this root
      <run-id>/
        run.json                   mb-run/2: identity, knobs, roster, solve,
                                   sim, events, base/best score
        grammar.yaml               the grammar this run was scored under
        pins.yaml                  the pin set that constrained it
        base/
          tournament.json          detector input, re-scorable
          framedata.json           detector input, re-scorable
          score.json               mb-score/2, verdict "base"
          overlay/data/...         the overlay stack the base already carried
        rounds/NNNN/
          candidate.json           mb-patch/2, verbatim
          patch/data/...           the candidate's overlay files
          tournament.json
          framedata.json
          score.json               mb-score/2 with the lineage block
        final/
          stack.json               mb-stack/2: heights, accepted, final score
          data/...                 the merged overlay of the accepted stack
          ledger.md                one tuning-ledger entry per accepted patch

Writes are atomic per file (write temp, rename), so an interrupted run leaves
a readable archive with status "running".

RE-SCORING is a first-class operation: the grammar is versioned and a patch
accepted under v3 may be rejected under v4, so lineages are recomputed rather
than grandfathered. `rescore()` re-runs the analysis over each round's STORED
detector inputs -- no matches, no solves, and it never writes.

THE EQUAL-HEIGHT ARCHIVE (`docs/balance-loop.md` section 4) is the pruning
rule: a lineage's height-N stack survives only if it OUTPERFORMS the best
archived height-N stack. Equal never survives -- the same strict-improvement
rule the round loop uses.

COMPARABILITY IS NOT ASSUMED. Two scores may be compared only when their
sim_version, grammar version, roster, seeds, solver version and solver backend
agree; the archive records that tuple per height and refuses to compare across
it rather than silently ranking incomparable numbers.
